    "summary": "Learn what an AI workflow is, why single prompts fail in production, the 4 levels of AI automation, and the 6 foundational pillars.",
    "content": "### 1. Lesson Title & Objective\n**Objective:** Learn what an AI workflow is, why single prompts fail in real applications, the 4 levels of AI capability, and the 6 foundational pillars.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Core Concepts Explained in Simple Words\n\n* **What an AI Workflow is:** An AI workflow is like a modern car factory assembly line. Instead of asking one worker to build an entire car alone, you break the job into small, organized stations. In software, Python code handles the conveyor belts, rules, and checks, while the AI model handles the thinking and writing.\n* **Why Single Prompts Fail:** If you give an AI one huge prompt with 10 complex tasks and a 100-page book:\n  * It suffers from **context limits** and forgets instructions in the middle.\n  * It makes up fake facts (**hallucinations**).\n  * It has **no memory**, so if step 9 fails, you must re-run the whole prompt from scratch and waste money.\n* **The 4 Levels of AI Capability:**\n  * **Level 1 (Single Q&A / Prompting):** One question in, one answer out. Zero memory or tools.\n  * **Level 2 (Chaining / Fixed Workflow):** Fixed steps connected in order. The output of Step 1 goes into Step 2.\n  * **Level 3 (Agentic Workflows):** The AI loops, thinks, calls tools, and checks its own work until done.\n  * **Level 4 (AI System):** Multiple specialized AI agents working together under a supervisor.\n* **The 6 Pillars of an AI Workflow:**\n  1. **LLM (The Brain):** Drafts text, reasons, and classifies user intent.\n  2. **Tools (Actions):** Runs code, calls external APIs, and queries databases.\n  3. **Memory (Saving Information):** Long-term storage that remembers user facts across weeks.\n  4. **State (The Relay Baton):** Short-term data passed between steps during one active run.\n  5. **Router (Control Flow):** The traffic cop that decides which branch to take next.\n  6. **Human-in-the-Loop (Approval Gate):** Stops the program and asks a real person to approve risky actions.\n* **Framework Overview:**\n  * **LlamaIndex:** Best for connecting to data and search (RAG).\n  * **LangChain:** Best for linking steps in a line (chains).\n  * **LangGraph:** Best for stateful loops, memory, and agents.\n  * **CrewAI / AutoGen:** Best for teams of multiple agents talking to each other.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Step-by-Step Architecture & Flow\n\n<AutomationStepper />\n\n```text\nTHE 6 PILLARS OF AN AI WORKFLOW\n               \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n               \u2502         1. Orchestrator Engine          \u2502\n               \u2502  (Coordinates steps and enforces rules) \u2502\n               \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n                                    \u2502\n           \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u253c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n           \u25bc                        \u25bc                        \u25bc\n\u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510   \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510   \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n\u2502      2. LLM        \u2502   \u2502     3. Tools       \u2502   \u2502 4. State & Memory  \u2502\n\u2502  (Thinks & drafts) \u2502   \u2502 (Database, APIs)   \u2502   \u2502 (Current & Past)   \u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518   \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518   \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n           \u2502                        \u2502                        \u2502\n           \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u253c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n                                    \u25bc\n               \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n               \u2502               5. Router                 \u2502\n               \u2502   (Decides what station to visit next)  \u2502\n               \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n                                    \u25bc\n               \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n               \u2502        6. Human-in-the-Loop             \u2502\n               \u2502   (Approval gate for high-risk actions) \u2502\n               \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4. Concrete Code Example\n\nHere is a clean 2-step workflow showing how Python code controls the flow:\n\n```python\n# A simple, clean 2-step workflow\ndef step1_extract_user_info(raw_text: str) -> dict:\n    # Deterministic code prepares the task\n    clean_text = raw_text.strip()\n    # Call the LLM for step 1 only\n    return {\"user_name\": \"Sophea\", \"intent\": \"billing_issue\"}\n\ndef step2_route_and_respond(state: dict) -> str:\n    # Router logic: check intent with simple Python code\n    if state[\"intent\"] == \"billing_issue\":\n        return f\"Hello {state['user_name']}, I am transferring you to the billing team.\"\n    return f\"Hello {state['user_name']}, how can I help you today?\"\n\n# Run the pipeline\nstate = step1_extract_user_info(\"My name is Sophea and my invoice is wrong.\")\nfinal_answer = step2_route_and_respond(state)\nprint(final_answer)\n```\n\n---\n\n### 5. 1-Minute Exam Review\n\n* **Workflows beat single prompts:** Workflows break big tasks into small, predictable steps that are easy to test and debug.\n* **4 Levels of Automation:** Level 1 (Single Prompt) $\to$ Level 2 (Chaining) $\to$ Level 3 (Agents with loops) $\to$ Level 4 (Multi-Agent platforms).\n* **State vs. Memory:** State is short-lived in-memory data for one run; Memory is permanent storage across user visits.\n* **Pillars Rule:** The LLM is the reasoning brain, but Python code and tools enforce business rules and safety gates."
