    "summary": "Compare local in-process tools against out-of-process MCP servers and navigate the 'Build Local First' decision path.",
    "content": "### 1. Lesson Title & Objective\n**Objective:** Compare local Python tools with out-of-process MCP servers, and learn the decision rule for when to migrate.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Core Concepts Explained in Simple Words\n\n* **The Pocket Calculator Analogy:**\n  * **A Local Tool** is like a pocket calculator: it's right in your pocket and gives answers in 0 milliseconds.\n  * **An MCP Server** is like calling the company accounting department: it takes a few seconds to connect, but they have their own computers, their own security vault, and any employee in the company can call them!\n* **Key Differences:**\n  * **Local Tools:** Run directly inside your Python app memory. Fast (sub-millisecond), simple to build, but locked to one codebase. If a local tool crashes, it can crash your whole app.\n  * **MCP Servers:** Run in a separate process or server. Complete fault isolation (if the server crashes, your AI host survives), polyglot (server can be in TypeScript, host in Python), and shared across multiple apps (Claude Desktop, Cursor, Custom Agent).\n* **The Migration Decision Rule:**\n  > *\"Build Local First. Migrate to MCP when justified.\"*  \n  Start with local Python functions for rapid prototyping. Only move to MCP when a second team or app needs to share the tool, or when you need separate security sandboxing.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Step-by-Step Architecture & Flow\n\n```text\nTHE ARCHITECTURAL DECISION PATH\nDoes this tool need to be shared across multiple apps or require isolated security?\n                       \u2502\n             \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n          NO \u2502                   \u2502 YES\n             \u25bc                   \u25bc\n    [ Build Local Tool ]   [ Build MCP Server ]\n    - In-process Python    - Out-of-process JSON-RPC\n    - Sub-millisecond      - Polyglot & isolated\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4. Concrete Code Example\n\nHere is a clean comparison of both approaches:\n\n```python\n# 1. Local Tool (In-Process Python function)\ndef local_tax_calc(amount: float) -> float:\n    return amount * 0.10  # 0.001ms latency, runs in current RAM\n\n# 2. Calling an MCP Server Tool (Out-of-Process over JSON-RPC)\n# payload = {\n#     \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n#     \"method\": \"tools/call\",\n#     \"params\": {\"name\": \"calculate_tax\", \"arguments\": {\"amount\": 100.0}},\n#     \"id\": 1\n# }\n# response = send_json_rpc(payload) # Isolated process, survivable crash\n```\n\n---\n\n### 5. 1-Minute Exam Review\n\n* **Local Tools:** In-process, sub-millisecond latency, simplest to deploy. Best for single-app prototypes.\n* **MCP Servers:** Out-of-process, fault-isolated, language-independent, reusable across multiple client apps.\n* **Fault Isolation:** A crash in a local tool crashes the agent; a crash in an MCP server only returns an error JSON message.\n* **Decision Rule:** Build local first; migrate to MCP only when multiple clients need the capability."
